 ],
 "literal": "Scooping the earth, curved, sharp-tipped horns / of majestic strong bulls, two together clashing, / the victorious one's raw hide, without scraping, covered / on the strongly bound drum, roaring in the middle of the battlefield, / in the intense battle-mingled field, causing distress, / a long spear pierced the honorable chest / approaching ... indeed, / with blood, the swaying chest, / giving no space for an embrace, swarmed the vultures.",
 "translation": "The war drum, covered with the unscraped raw hide of a majestic bull that won a clash against another sharp-horned bull, roars in the middle of the battlefield. In that terrifying, chaotic battle, a long spear has pierced the chest of a brave, honorable warrior. On his chest, where blood flows and sways, vultures have swarmed so thickly that they leave no room even for his wife's embrace.",
 "modern": "மண்ணைத் தோண்டும் வளைந்த கூர்மையான கொம்புகளையுடைய இரண்டு பெருமைமிக்க காளைகள் ஒன்றுடன் ஒன்று சண்டையிட்டு, அதில் வென்ற காளையின் பதப்படுத்தப்படாத தோலால் போர்த்தப்பட்ட வலிமையான முரசு போர்க்களத்தின் நடுவே முழங்குகிறது. அத்தகைய அஞ்சத்தக்க போர்க்களத்தில், நீண்ட வேல் பாய்ந்ததால் மானமுள்ள நெஞ்சையும் குருதி வழியும் மார்பையும் உடைய அந்த வீரனை, அவனது மனைவி தழுவுவதற்குக் கூட இடமில்லாமல் பருந்துகள் மொய்த்துக் கொண்டிருக்கின்றன.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Tumpai tiṇai (warfare) and the theme of Mūtiṉ mullai, which extols the bravery of ancient warrior families. The poet describes a fierce battlefield where a war drum, covered with the raw hide of a victorious bull, roars loudly. A brave warrior has been pierced through his chest by a long spear, and he lies dying on the field. His chest is covered in blood, and vultures have swarmed over him so densely that his wife cannot even find space to embrace him one last time. The imagery of the clashing bulls and the raw-hide drum sets a tone of primal, heroic violence, while the vultures crowding out the wife's embrace highlights the tragic, stark reality of a hero's death.",
